**Heart failure** means your heart is **not pumping blood around your body as well as it should**. It is usually a long-term condition that gets worse over time, but treatment can control symptoms for many years. Around **1 million people** in the UK have heart failure.

## Symptoms

- **breathlessness**, when active, lying flat or at night
- feeling very **tired** and weak
- **swollen ankles and legs**, caused by fluid building up
- sudden **weight gain**
- feeling dizzy or light-headed

## Common causes

- a **heart attack** or **coronary heart disease**
- **high blood pressure**
- **atrial fibrillation** and other heart rhythm problems
- problems with the heart valves or heart muscle (cardiomyopathy)
- heavy drinking

## Getting diagnosed

Your GP may do a blood test called **NT-proBNP**. If it is raised, you will be referred for an **echocardiogram**, an ultrasound scan of the heart. It shows how well your heart pumps, often given as the **ejection fraction**.

Read about [heart failure treatment](/conditions/heart-and-circulation/heart-failure/treatment/).

{% callout type="danger" title="Get help quickly" %}
Call **999** if you have chest pain, severe breathlessness, or you cough up pink, frothy mucus. Contact your heart failure team or **NHS 111** if you are more breathless than usual, your ankles swell more, or you gain weight quickly.
{% /callout %}
